  "stable_key": "g5-lesson-06",
  "position": 1,
  "title": "Volume and surface area of pyramid, cone and sphere",
  "summary": "Distinguish perpendicular height, slant height and radius, then calculate volumes and exposed surface areas of pyramids, cones, spheres and composite solids.",
  "outcomes": [
    "5.6"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [],
  "estimated_minutes": 85,
  "objectives": [
    "Choose the correct dimensions and formula for a pyramid, cone or sphere.",
    "Calculate volume, curved surface area and total exposed surface area.",
    "Solve reverse and composite-solid problems with consistent units and suitable rounding."
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-56-dimensions",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Read the solid before choosing a formula",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Mensuration questions often provide several lengths, but each formula uses particular dimensions. The radius runs from the centre of a circular base to its edge, so it is half the diameter. A perpendicular height meets the base at 90° and is used in every volume formula. A slant height lies on a triangular or curved face and is used for surface area."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Volume measures space inside a solid and uses cubic units. Surface area measures the exposed covering and uses square units. Sketch the solid, label r, h and l, and decide whether the question asks for inside space, curved covering, or every exposed face before substituting."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-56-pyramid",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Pyramids: one third of the matching prism",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A pyramid has a polygon base and triangular faces meeting at an apex. Its volume is one third of the volume of a prism with the same base area and perpendicular height. For surface area, calculate the base and each triangular face separately. A triangular face area uses the perpendicular height within that face, which is usually a slant height rather than the pyramid’s vertical height."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "V=\\frac13 A_{\\text{base}}h"
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g5-lesson-06-pyramid"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-56-pyramid-example",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: square-based pyramid",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A square-based pyramid has base side 10 cm, perpendicular height 12 cm and slant height 13 cm on every triangular face. Find its volume and total surface area."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The base area is 10² = 100 cm². Use the perpendicular height for volume."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "V=\\frac13(100)(12)=400\\text{ cm}^3"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Each triangular face has area ½ × 10 × 13 = 65 cm². There are four equal faces."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "S=100+4(65)=360\\text{ cm}^2"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The volume is one third of the 1,200 cm³ matching prism, and the surface area includes exactly one base plus four triangular faces."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-56-pyramid-reverse-example",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: find a height from the volume",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A pyramid has a rectangular base 8 cm by 6 cm and volume 224 cm³. Find its perpendicular height."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The base area is 8 × 6 = 48 cm². Substitute into the volume formula."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "224=\\frac13(48)h"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Simplify, then divide by 16."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "224=16h\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad h=14\\text{ cm}"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "Substitution gives ⅓ × 48 × 14 = 224 cm³. Without the one-third, the height would come out three times too small."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-56-cone",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Cones: keep vertical and slant height separate",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A cone is one third of the matching cylinder, so its volume uses the vertical height h. Its curved surface unwraps to a sector and has area πrl, where l is the slant height. Add the circular base πr² only when the base is exposed. In a right cone, r, h and l form a right triangle, so l² = r² + h²."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "V=\\frac13\\pi r^2h,\\qquad A_{\\text{curved}}=\\pi rl"
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g5-lesson-06-cone"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-56-cone-example",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: find a cone’s slant height first",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A closed cone has radius 4 cm and perpendicular height 7.5 cm. Find its volume and total surface area, giving answers to 1 decimal place."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Use the right triangle through the centre to find the slant height."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "l=\\sqrt{4^2+7.5^2}=\\sqrt{72.25}=8.5\\text{ cm}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "V=\\frac13\\pi(4)^2(7.5)=40\\pi\\approx125.7\\text{ cm}^3"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The cone is closed, so include the curved area and its base."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "S=\\pi(4)(8.5)+\\pi(4)^2=34\\pi+16\\pi=50\\pi\\approx157.1\\text{ cm}^2"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The slant height 8.5 cm is longer than both perpendicular sides, and the total area exceeds the curved area 34π cm²."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-56-sphere",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Spheres and hemispheres",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Every point on a sphere is the same distance r from its centre. The volume formula contains r³, while surface area contains r², so confusing radius with diameter causes a large error. A hemisphere has half the sphere’s volume and half its curved area. Its total surface area also includes the flat circular base."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "V_{\\text{sphere}}=\\frac43\\pi r^3,\\qquad S_{\\text{sphere}}=4\\pi r^2"
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g5-lesson-06-sphere"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-56-sphere-example",
      "position": 8,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: hemisphere container",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "An open hemispherical bowl has internal diameter 18 cm. Find its capacity and the curved internal area, giving answers to 1 decimal place."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The radius is 9 cm. Capacity is the hemisphere’s internal volume."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "V=\\frac12\\left(\\frac43\\pi(9)^3\\right)=486\\pi\\approx1526.8\\text{ cm}^3"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The bowl is open, so no flat circular base is included."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "A=2\\pi(9)^2=162\\pi\\approx508.9\\text{ cm}^2"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The hemisphere’s results are half the corresponding sphere results, and the radius used is half the stated diameter."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-56-composite",
      "position": 9,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Composite solids and exposed surfaces",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For a composite solid, add volumes of joined pieces and subtract volumes of holes or removed pieces. For surface area, count only surfaces exposed to air, liquid, paint or wrapping. A circular join between a cone and hemisphere is internal, so it is excluded from the outside area. Write a short surface inventory before calculating."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Convert all lengths to one unit before using formulas. Since 1 m = 100 cm, 1 m² = 10,000 cm² and 1 m³ = 1,000,000 cm³. Keep π and unrounded intermediate values on the calculator, then round once at the end and attach the correct square or cubic unit."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-56-composite-example",
      "position": 10,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: cone on a hemisphere",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A solid is made by joining a cone of radius 4 cm and perpendicular height 3 cm to a hemisphere of radius 4 cm, flat face to flat face. Find its volume and its exposed surface area in terms of π."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Volume: add the two parts."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "V=\\frac13\\pi(4)^2(3)+\\frac23\\pi(4)^3=16\\pi+\\frac{128}{3}\\pi=\\frac{176}{3}\\pi\\text{ cm}^3"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Surface: the circular join is inside the solid, so only the two curved surfaces are exposed. The cone’s slant height is √(4² + 3²) = 5 cm."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "S=\\pi(4)(5)+2\\pi(4)^2=20\\pi+32\\pi=52\\pi\\text{ cm}^2"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "As decimals, V ≈ 184.3 cm³ and S ≈ 163.4 cm². Neither flat circle is counted, because both lie on the join."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-56-similar-solids",
      "position": 11,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Similar solids: area and volume scale factors",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Two solids are similar when one is an enlargement of the other: every length is multiplied by the same scale factor k. An area is found by multiplying two lengths, so every area, including surface area, is multiplied by k². A volume is found by multiplying three lengths, so the volume is multiplied by k³."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "\\text{length}\\times k,\\qquad\\text{area}\\times k^2,\\qquad\\text{volume}\\times k^3"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Liquid in a cone held vertex down forms a smaller cone that is similar to the container, because its sloping sides lie along the container’s sides. If you know what fraction of the volume is filled, take the cube root to find the length scale factor. In the same way, if the radius of a sphere increases by 10%, then k = 1.1 and the volume is multiplied by 1.1³ = 1.331, an increase of 33.1%."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-56-similar-example",
      "position": 12,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: depth of liquid in a cone",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A cone-shaped container of height 24 cm is held vertex down. Water fills 27/64 of its volume and forms a smaller cone at the tip. Find the depth of the water."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The water cone is similar to the container, so its volume is k³ times the full volume, where k is the ratio of the depths."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "k^3=\\frac{27}{64}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{27}{64}}=\\frac34"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\text{depth}=\\frac34\\times24=18\\text{ cm}"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "Check: (18 ÷ 24)³ = (3/4)³ = 27/64. The water is three quarters of the way up, although less than half the volume is filled, because the cone is narrow at the tip."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-56-errors",
      "position": 13,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Common errors to check",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Before giving an answer, check for these errors: using the diameter as r; using the slant height l in a volume formula, or the vertical height h in πrl; adding a circular base to an open cone or bowl; forgetting the one-third in a pyramid or cone volume; and multiplying a volume by k instead of k³."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-56-recall",
      "position": 14,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A sphere has diameter 12 cm. Enter its surface area as a multiple of π; for example, enter 20 for 20π."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "144",
        "canonical_latex": "144\\pi\\text{ cm}^2",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The radius is 6 cm, so 4πr² = 4π(6²) = 144π cm²."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-06-summary",
      "position": 15,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Mensuration checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Mark radius versus diameter and vertical height versus slant height. Choose volume or surface area, list the exposed faces, and convert lengths before substituting."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use cubic units for volume and square units for area. Retain π and full calculator precision until the final line, round only as requested, and check that a one-third or one-half factor has not been missed."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For similar solids with length scale factor k, multiply areas by k² and volumes by k³; to go from a volume ratio back to a length ratio, take the cube root."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
